===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|frustum||morsel}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-us-frustum.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|frusta}}

# A [[cone]] or [[pyramid]] whose [[tip]] has been [[truncated]] by a [[plane]] [[parallel]] to its [[base]].
#* '''1742''', Colin MacLaurin, ''A Treatise of Fluxions'', Volume 1, [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=dCAOAAAAQAAJ&pg=RA1-PA15&dq=%22frustum%22%7C%22frustums%22%7C%22frusta%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=vImhU-H_DI3y8QW_9YDoDQ&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22frustum%22%7C%22frustums%22%7C%22frusta%22&f=false page 25],
#*: In a parabolic conoid this difference vaniſhes, the '''fruſtum''' being always equal to a cylinder of the ſame height upon the ſection of the conoid that biſects the altitude of the '''fruſtum''' and is parallel to its baſes.
#* {{RQ:British Encyclopedia|volume=III|url=https://archive.org/details/gri_33125014817791/page/n259/mode/1up|passage=This theorem holds good for complete solids as well as '''frustums''', whether right or oblique, and not only of the solids generated from the conic sections, but also of all pyramids, cones, and in short of any solid, whose parallel sections are similar figures.}}
#* '''1974''', {{w|Stanisław Lem}}, trans. {{w|Michael Kandel}}, ''{{w|The Cyberiad}}'':
#*: Come, every '''frustum''' longs to be a cone,<br>And every vector dreams of matrices.
#* '''2006''', Pawan Harish Nirnimesh, P. J. Narayanan, ''Culling an Object Hierarchy to a '''Frustum''' Hierarchy'', Prem Kalra, Shmuel Peleg (editors), ''Computer Vision, Graphics and Image Processing: 5th Indian Conference, ICVGIP 2006'', Springer, LNCS4338, [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=HaWij_nMKsMC&pg=PA252&dq=%22frustums%22%7C%22frusta%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=I5ehU5uPEcmHlAXKuIDQCw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22frustums%22%7C%22frusta%22&f=false page 252],
#*: However, when there are multiple view '''frustums''' (as in a tiled display wall), visibility culling time becomes substantial and cannot be hidden by pipelining it with other stages of rendering.
#* '''2008''', R. Benjamin Davis, ''Techniques to Assess Acoustic-structure Interaction in Liquid Rocket Engines'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=FSxVyL9-xBYC&pg=PA122&dq=%22frusta%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=ZJqhU-r4FtTj8AXf8oLwBw&ved=0CIYBEOgBMBI#v=onepage&q=%22frusta%22&f=false page 122],
#*: Here, the dynamics of the fluid-filled '''frusta''' of cones are considered (see Figure 5.5). The '''frusta''' are clamped at their roots and free at their ends.
# A portion of a [[sphere]], or in general any [[solid]], [[delimit]]ed by two [[parallel]] [[plane]]s.
#* '''1840''', James Blundell, ''Observations on Some of the More Important Diseases of Women'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=a8EwAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA131&dq=%22frustum%22%7C%22frustums%22%7C%22frusta%22+sphere&hl=en&sa=X&ei=wIiiU6-xPIKzlQWKoIDIBA&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22frustum%22%7C%22frustums%22%7C%22frusta%22%20sphere&f=false page 131],
#*: In some women it<sup>[the os uteri]</sup> is flat, in many more tuberose, and forming, as it were, a '''frustum''' of a sphere;{{...}}.
#* '''2014''', John Bird, ''Engineering Mathematics'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=_BhgAwAAQBAJ&pg=PA183&dq=%22frustum%22%7C%22frustums%22%7C%22frusta%22+sphere&hl=en&sa=X&ei=wIiiU6-xPIKzlQWKoIDIBA&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22frustum%22%7C%22frustums%22%7C%22frusta%22%20sphere&f=false page 183],
#*: '''Problem 22.''' Determine the volume of a '''frustum''' of a sphere of diameter 49.74 cm if the diameter{{sic}} of the ends of the '''frustum''' are 24.0 and 40.0 cm, and the height of the '''frustum''' is 7.00 cm.

====Usage notes====
The misspelling {{m|en|frustrum}} is by incorrect analogy with {{m|en|frustrate}}, also of Latin origin.<ref>{{R:OED}}</ref>
* {{sense|portion of a sphere}} The portion of the [[surface]] of a sphere delimited by parallel planes (i.e., the curved surface of a frustum) may be called a {{m|en|zone}}; however, that term is also sometimes used as a synonym of '''''frustum'''''.

