==Latin==

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*bʰrews-}}
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*frustom}}, from {{inh|la|ine-pro||*bʰrus-tós}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*bʰrews-||to break up, cut}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|frū̆stum}}

The etymology is consistent with the /u/ in the first syllable being short, and the word is shown without a macron in De Vaan's dictionary.<ref>{{R:itc:EDL|head=frustum|page=245}}</ref> Although Bennett 1907 says "ū acc. to the Romance"<ref>{{cite-book|author=Charles E. Bennett|title=The Latin Language – a historical outline of its sounds, inflections, and syntax|chapter=Hidden Quantity|location=Boston|publisher=Allyn and Bacon|year=1907|page=59|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/latinlanguagehis00bennuoft/page/59|authorlink=Charles E. Bennett (scholar)}}</ref>, there are related words in Romance that point to short u in the descendants of the derivative  *{{m|la|Reconstruction:Latin/frustio|frŭstiāre}}<ref>{{R:FEW|3|831|*frustiare}}</ref>, such as Old French ''froissier.''

